WEBVTT

00:00:00.050 --> 00:00:02.636
Three hundred and fifty-seven billion dollars.

00:00:02.636 --> 00:00:05.375
That is Canada's total infrastructure deficit.

00:00:05.375 --> 00:00:10.409
Thirty percent of municipal assets are in fair, poor, or very poor condition.

00:00:10.409 --> 00:00:13.500
Forty percent of roads are in fair or poor condition.

00:00:13.500 --> 00:00:16.397
Twenty-six percent of bridges need major repair.

00:00:16.397 --> 00:00:21.602
The Ottawa LRT alone has cost over two point one billion dollars including remediation.

00:00:21.602 --> 00:00:26.340
Thirty-three long-term drinking water advisories remained active in 2024.

00:00:26.340 --> 00:00:30.340
The breakdown: one hundred and fifteen billion for roads and highways.

00:00:30.340 --> 00:00:32.738
Eighty billion for water and wastewater.

00:00:32.738 --> 00:00:35.022
Sixty billion for transit systems.

00:00:35.022 --> 00:00:37.670
Fifty billion for bridges and overpasses.

00:00:37.670 --> 00:00:40.022
Fifty-two billion for public buildings.

00:00:40.022 --> 00:00:46.386
Five point nine billion dollars in infrastructure funding lapses annually — money allocated but never spent.

00:00:46.386 --> 00:00:51.204
The generation that won World War II built infrastructure designed to last eighty years.

00:00:51.204 --> 00:00:55.147
Modern Canada cannot maintain what they built, let alone replace it.
